# Zerg Grammar
#
# The formal surface grammar of the Zerg language, written in W3C-style EBNF and
# built up one grammar group at a time, from core to minor. This file is the single
# source of truth for what is syntactically well-formed; the prose companion lives in
# docs/grammar.md (and docs/grammar.zh-TW.md).
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Notation (W3C-style EBNF)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   name ::= ...     a production; `name` is a non-terminal (lower-kebab-case)
#   'text'           a literal terminal, matched verbatim
#   A B              A followed by B (concatenation)
#   A | B            A or B (alternation)
#   ( A )            grouping
#   A?               zero or one A
#   A*               zero or more A
#   A+               one or more A
#   [a-z]            a character in the given range
#   [^x]             any character except x
#   < text >         an informal terminal, described in prose
#   UPPER-CASE       a lexical token defined under the Lexical group
#   #                a comment, to end of line (same as Zerg's own comment syntax)
#
# ==================================================================================
# 1. nop & program skeleton
# ==================================================================================
# The smallest well-formed Zerg program is a sequence of statements. The first
# statement the grammar defines is `nop` — the placeholder for an empty statement:
# it does nothing and yields nothing, standing in wherever a statement is required
# but none is wanted (an empty function body `fn f() { nop }`, an empty loop
# `for { nop }`). Later groups extend `statement` with the other statement forms.
#
# A statement is separated from the next by a line break or a ';'. Both are
# GRAMMATICALLY VALID, but the formatter normalizes a multi-statement line into
# one statement per line — so canonical Zerg has exactly one statement per line
# and ';' rarely survives formatting. (';' also appears inside array types and
# literals `[T; N]`, an unrelated position that formatting keeps.)

program   ::= stmt-list
stmt-list ::= stmt-sep* ( statement ( stmt-sep+ statement )* stmt-sep* )?
stmt-sep  ::= NEWLINE | ';'       # ';' is valid but the formatter splits onto lines

# A statement is either simple (no block — fits on one line), compound (it owns a
# '{ … }' block), or a declaration (introduces a name, and may carry a decorator
# prefix). 'nop' is the smallest simple statement; the rest are filled in by later
# groups.
statement      ::= simple-stmt | compound-stmt | decorated-decl
simple-stmt    ::= nop | binding | reassign | print | return | raise | break
                 | continue | spawn-stmt | send-stmt | import-stmt | defer-stmt
                 | del-stmt | expr-stmt
compound-stmt  ::= block | with-stmt | if-stmt | for-stmt | select-stmt

# A declaration introduces a name — a type, a function, or a module item. A DECORATOR
# prefix (group 7) may lead ANY declaration and binds to it; which decorators are
# valid on which declaration is a SEMANTIC rule, not a syntactic one (e.g. 'derive'
# only on 'struct'/'enum', a logging decorator only on 'fn'). 'type-decl' is the one
# declaration with no block; the rest own one.
decorated-decl ::= decorator* declaration
declaration    ::= type-decl | fn-decl | struct-decl | enum-decl
                 | spec-decl | impl-decl | init-decl
nop            ::= 'nop'

# ==================================================================================
# 2. Lexical
# ==================================================================================
# Source is UTF-8. Horizontal whitespace (space, tab) separates tokens and is
# otherwise insignificant. A line break is significant: the lexer turns it into a
# ';' separator (ASI, below). Comments are not tokens.
#
# Newlines & ASI (automatic ';' insertion). At a line break the lexer inserts a ';'
# when the last token on the line can END an item — an identifier, a literal, ')',
# ']', '}', '?', '_', 'this', or one of 'return' 'break' 'continue' 'nop'. It does
# NOT insert one inside an unclosed '(' or '[', so an expression or type may span
# lines there. This single rule makes statements, struct fields, enum variants, and
# match arms all newline-separated with the same ';' separator. ',' separates the
# elements of a value list instead — arguments, tuples, generics, a variant payload,
# and the fields of a struct pattern/literal (a composite, like Go's Point{X: 1, Y: 2}).
#
# Decorators & '#'. '#[' begins a DECORATOR — a compiler directive (group 7); any
# other '#' begins a line comment. The lexer peeks one character, so the two never
# collide. A decorator binds to the declaration that follows it; a line break between
# a decorator and its target does not separate them (no ASI ';' applies there).

letter     ::= [a-zA-Z]
digit      ::= [0-9]

# An identifier names a binding, function, type, field, ...; a reserved keyword is
# never an identifier.
identifier ::= ( letter | '_' ) ( letter | digit | '_' )*

NEWLINE    ::= '\n'                # a line break; terminates a statement (group 1)
WS         ::= ( ' ' | '\t' )+     # horizontal whitespace between tokens
COMMENT    ::= '#' [^[\n] [^\n]*   # a '#' NOT followed by '[' → comment to EOL
             | '#' NEWLINE          # a bare '#' at end of line is also a comment

# A block groups a statement list in braces — the body of a function, loop, or
# conditional, and ALSO a value: a block is an EXPRESSION (primary, group 4) whose VALUE
# is its last statement's value — an expr-stmt yields its expr; any other statement, or
# an empty block, yields 'nil'. The ASI ';' only SEPARATES statements; it does not
# discard a value the way a trailing ';' does in some languages. So 'guard { … }' and a
# 'match' arm can hold many statements and still yield. Inner separators follow group 1.
block      ::= '{' stmt-list '}'

# The reserved keywords, listed as a lexical fact: none can be an identifier. Each
# word's grammar is defined by the group that introduces it.
keyword    ::= 'nop'   | 'fn'    | 'mut'    | 'pub'     | 'return' | 'import'
             | 'if'    | 'else'  | 'for'    | 'in'      | 'break'  | 'continue'
             | 'match' | 'spawn' | 'select' | 'struct'  | 'enum'   | 'spec'
             | 'chan'  | 'type'  | 'impl'   | 'package' | 'init'  | 'extern'
             | 'defer' | 'del'   | 'raise'  | 'guard'   | 'is'    | 'not'
             | 'and'   | 'or'    | 'print'  | 'this'    | 'with'  | 'as'
             | 'from'  | 'true'  | 'false'  | 'nil'
# ('derive' is not a keyword — it is a compiler decorator name, '#[derive(...)]'.)

# ==================================================================================
# 3. Literals
# ==================================================================================
# A literal denotes a constant value. A numeric literal is untyped — it adopts the
# type its context demands (an integer literal defaults to `int`, a fractional or
# exponent literal to `float`). A '_' may group digits for readability, between
# digits only (never leading, trailing, or doubled).

literal     ::= bool-lit | nil-lit | float-lit | int-lit
              | rune-lit | byte-lit | str-lit | raw-str-lit

bool-lit    ::= 'true' | 'false'
nil-lit     ::= 'nil'                      # the placeholder value of a T?

# Integers: decimal or a based form. Sign is not part of the literal (unary '-' is
# an operator, group 4).
int-lit     ::= dec-int | hex-int | oct-int | bin-int
dec-int     ::= digit ( '_'? digit )*
hex-int     ::= '0x' hex-digit ( '_'? hex-digit )*
oct-int     ::= '0o' oct-digit ( '_'? oct-digit )*
bin-int     ::= '0b' bin-digit ( '_'? bin-digit )*
hex-digit   ::= [0-9a-fA-F]
oct-digit   ::= [0-7]
bin-digit   ::= [0-1]

# Floats: a fractional part, an exponent, or both (a bare integer is int-lit).
float-lit   ::= dec-int '.' dec-int exponent?
              | dec-int exponent
exponent    ::= ( 'e' | 'E' ) ( '+' | '-' )? dec-int

# Text. A rune is one Unicode code point in single quotes; a byte is one octet,
# 'b'-prefixed (or `byte(0x41)` by cast, group 7). A str is double-quoted and processes
# escapes; a TRIPLE-quoted '"""..."""' str is the same but spans lines (line breaks are
# literal, and a lone '"' or '""' inside needs no escape — only '"""' ends it); a raw str
# is 'r'-prefixed and processes none. A str may not hold a NUL, so '\0' and '\u{0}' are
# invalid inside "..." (but valid in a rune or byte). f"..." interpolation is deferred to
# the expression group.
rune-lit    ::= "'" ( rune-char | escape ) "'"
byte-lit    ::= 'b' "'" ( byte-char | byte-escape ) "'"
str-lit     ::= '"' ( str-char | escape )* '"'
              | '"""' ( ml-str-char | escape )* '"""'    # multiline; line breaks are literal
raw-str-lit ::= 'r' '"' raw-char* '"'

escape      ::= '\' ( 'n' | 't' | 'r' | '0' | '\' | "'" | '"' | 'u' '{' hex-digit+ '}' )
byte-escape ::= '\' ( 'n' | 't' | 'r' | '0' | '\' | "'" | 'x' hex-digit hex-digit )
rune-char   ::= < any code point except "'", '\', or a line break >
byte-char   ::= < any octet 0x00..0x7F except "'", '\', or a line break >
str-char    ::= < any code point except '"', '\', NUL, or a line break >
ml-str-char ::= < any code point except '\', NUL, or a closing '"""' >   # line breaks allowed
raw-char    ::= < any code point except '"' or a line break >   # no escapes

# ==================================================================================
# 4. Bindings & Expressions
# ==================================================================================
# A binding introduces a name: ':=' is immutable, 'mut … :=' rebindable, '=' reassigns
# an existing lvalue. An expression on its own is a statement (a call, or a match run
# for effect). Destructuring a pattern at ':=' arrives with patterns (group 6).

binding      ::= 'mut'? bind-target ':=' expr
bind-target  ::= identifier | tuple-pat | struct-pat   # destructuring: group 6
reassign     ::= assign-target '=' expr
expr-stmt    ::= expr
lvalue       ::= identifier ( '.' identifier | '.' dec-int | '[' expr ']' )*   # '.0' = tuple element
# An assignment target is an lvalue, or a tuple / struct SHAPE whose leaves are lvalues —
# destructuring reassignment, the mirror of a ':=' bind-target but into EXISTING names
# ('(a, b) = e', 'Div{q, r} = e'); leaves may be any lvalue ('(a, obj.f) = e').
assign-target ::= lvalue
               | '(' assign-target ( ',' assign-target )* ')'
               | type-name '{' field-target ( ',' field-target )* ( ',' '..' )? '}'
field-target  ::= identifier ( ':' assign-target )?

# Expression grammar from loosest ('??') to tightest (postfix). Binary levels are
# left-associative; comparison is non-associative (no a < b < c chaining). A RANGE '..'
# binds tighter than comparison so 'v in 0..10' reads 'v in (0..10)'; membership 'in'
# and the type test 'is' sit at the comparison level.
expr         ::= coalesce-expr          # '??' is the loosest binary (group 8)
or-expr      ::= and-expr   ( 'or'  and-expr  )*
and-expr     ::= cmp-expr   ( 'and' cmp-expr  )*
cmp-expr     ::= range-expr ( ( cmp-op range-expr ) | ( 'is' type-name ) | ( 'in' range-expr ) )?
range-expr   ::= add-expr ( '..' add-expr? | '..=' add-expr )?
             # 'x..y' → range(x, y); 'x..=y' → range(x, y+1); 'x..' → an open range. 'v in r'
             # → r.contains(v). Both are SUGAR (the Range/contains machinery is stdlib).
add-expr     ::= mul-expr   ( add-op mul-expr )*
mul-expr     ::= unary-expr ( mul-op unary-expr )*
unary-expr   ::= unary-op unary-expr | postfix-expr
postfix-expr ::= recv-base ( postfix )*
recv-base    ::= '<-' recv-base | primary   # '<-ch' receive (group 9); '(<-ch)?' etc.
primary      ::= literal | fstr-lit | fn-expr | match-expr | guard-expr | if-expr
               | chan-new | list-lit | map-lit | tuple-lit | block
               | identifier | '(' expr ')'

postfix      ::= '.' identifier        # field or method access
               | '.' dec-int           # tuple element by STATIC index — 't.0', 't.1' (0-based)
               | '(' arg-list? ')'     # call (named arguments: group 5)
               | '[' expr ']'          # index — a single-value subscript
               | type-args             # explicit type arguments 'f[K, V]' (type-args, group 7)
               | '?'                   # propagate (group 8): unwrap Left, else early-return Right
               | '!'                   # force-unwrap (group 8): unwrap Left, else raise UnwrapError
               | '?.' identifier       # optional chain (group 8): read .id when present, else nil
arg-list     ::= arg ( ',' arg )*
arg          ::= ( identifier ':' )? expr   # positional, or named 'name: value' (g5)

# NAME RESOLUTION disambiguates two surfaces that share '[ … ]'. A single '[X]' is an
# INDEX when the base resolves to a value, and TYPE ARGUMENTS when the base resolves to
# a generic function or a type constructor ('parse[int]', 'list[int]') — a name is
# exactly one of these (a type and a function cannot share a name, group 7). A comma —
# '[X, Y]' — is unambiguously type arguments (an index takes one value). This is the
# SAME name resolution the pattern grammar uses to tell a variant from a binding
# (group 6): the grammar is resolved with scope in hand, not purely context-free.

# Composite literals build values in expression position. A tuple is a parenthesized
# 2+ list — a single '( expr )' is just grouping. A list is bracketed. A map is braced
# with ':'-bearing entries: that ':' is exactly what distinguishes a '{ … }' map from a
# block, so a bare-element '{ … }' is ALWAYS a block and a set therefore has NO brace
# literal (build one with its constructor, 'set([ … ])'). Two parse rules follow from '{'
# being the block opener: at a statement's start '{' is a block STATEMENT (its value
# discarded), and ANY '{'-opening expression — a block OR a map literal — at the start of
# an if/for/with/match head must be parenthesized. (struct and enum values are built by
# calling a type or variant name — see the constructor note in group 7.)
#
# A tuple is read back by STATIC index — 't.0', 't.1' (postfix, above) — not 't[i]': its
# elements are heterogeneous, so the index must be a compile-time constant for the result
# type to be known. In this position a following number is a bare index, so 'a.0.1' is
# '(a.0).1', not a float. (A list/map, being homogeneous, uses the runtime 't[i]' index.)
tuple-lit    ::= '(' expr ',' expr ( ',' expr )* ')'    # 2+ elements; no 1-tuple, no '()'
list-lit     ::= '[' ( expr ( ',' expr )* )? ']'        # [1, 2, 3]; empty '[]'
map-lit      ::= '{' map-entry ( ',' map-entry )* '}'   # {k: v, …}
             | '{' ':' '}'                               # empty map '{:}'
map-entry    ::= expr ':' expr

cmp-op       ::= '==' | '!=' | '<' | '>' | '<=' | '>='
add-op       ::= '+' | '-' | '+%' | '-%' | '|' | '^'
mul-op       ::= '*' | '/' | '%' | '*%' | '<<' | '>>' | '&'
unary-op     ::= 'not' | '~' | '-' | '-%'

type-name    ::= identifier            # a spec or enum-variant name (groups 6, 7)

# ==================================================================================
# 5. Functions
# ==================================================================================
# A function is a first-class value. A declaration binds a name (top level, or a
# module item); an anonymous function is an expression — a closure over immutable
# captures. 'return' exits, optionally with a value; an absent '-> type' means the
# function returns nil. A parameter passes BY VALUE (a copy); 'mut &x' instead passes a
# mutable reference (below). A parameter may carry a default; a call fills parameters
# positionally, then by name. ('type' is defined in group 7 (Types); referenced here.)

fn-decl      ::= 'pub'? 'mut'? 'fn' identifier generics? '(' param-list? ')' ret-type? block
fn-expr      ::= 'fn' '(' param-list? ')' ret-type? block        # anonymous / closure — never generic
fn-type      ::= 'fn' '(' param-type-list? ')' ret-type?         # a function's type

# 'mut fn' marks a METHOD that mutates its implicit receiver 'this' in place — exactly the
# 'mut &this' case. It is meaningful only on a method (a 'fn' in an 'impl' or 'spec'); the
# call site must hold the receiver in a 'mut' binding. A free function or closure has no
# receiver, so it is never 'mut fn'. This tracks mutation of the value's own Zerg-owned
# fields — not effects on a resource behind a Ref[T]/foreign handle, which need no 'mut'.
ret-type     ::= '->' type
return       ::= 'return' expr?

# PARAMETERS pass BY VALUE (a copy). 'mut &x' passes a MUTABLE REFERENCE: the callee may
# change x and the change AFFECTS the caller's argument. Two independent controls meet —
# the CALLER decides whether its variable is 'mut', the CALLEE decides via 'mut &' whether
# it writes back — so a caller-visible mutation needs BOTH, and the argument must be a
# 'mut' lvalue. There is NO call-site marker: the signature is the contract. A 'mut &'
# reference is valid only for the call — it cannot ESCAPE (be captured by a spawn or
# stored past the call) and cannot ALIAS (the same variable to two 'mut &' in one call),
# which keeps it safe with no borrow checker. There is no plain 'mut x' parameter; for a
# private mutable copy, shadow — 'mut x := x'.
param-list   ::= param ( ',' param )*
param        ::= ( 'mut' '&' )? identifier ':' type ( '=' expr )?   # 'mut &x' = mutable reference
param-type-list ::= param-type ( ',' param-type )*
param-type   ::= ( 'mut' '&' )? type

# 'this' is NOT a parameter. Inside a method (a 'fn' declared in an 'impl' or a
# 'spec') 'this' is the implicit receiver, always reachable through the instance
# the method is called on. A 'fn' whose body uses 'this' with no instance bound to
# it is a compile error (a free function has no receiver). The self type is 'This'.

# A call's named arguments are the 'arg' form above (group 4): positional arguments
# come first, then any argument may be given by name; once one is named, the rest
# must be named too. A default lets a named argument be omitted.
#
# ARGUMENT CONVENTIONS. Names are resolved at COMPILE time against the signature, so a
# 'map' does NOT splat into named arguments — its keys are runtime strings and its values
# one homogeneous type, while parameters are compile-time and heterogeneous. There are
# also no VARIADICS. Pass a 'list' for many positional values, and an OPTIONS STRUCT with
# field defaults for a bag of named options ('draw(Style(width: 2))') — the statically
# typed stand-in for keyword arguments. A 'map' is passed as an ordinary value, never
# expanded into a call's names.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# String interpolation & print (the formatting / output surface)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# An f-string embeds '{ … }' holes, joined with the literal text; '{{' and '}}' are
# literal braces. Python-style, a hole is 'expr' then optional '=' (self-documenting:
# emits the expression's source text and '='), a '!' conversion, and a ':' format
# spec. Semantics: 'expr' → display(); '!r'/'!s'/'!a' → debug/display/ascii first;
# ':spec' → expr.format(spec), a per-type Format protocol (the spec string's meaning
# is the type's — stdlib numbers/str read the usual fill/align/sign/#/0/width/.prec/
# type). 'print' writes a value's display() and a newline to stdout — a reserved
# keyword, always in scope, best-effort (never raises).
fstr-lit     ::= 'f' '"' ( fstr-char | escape | '{{' | '}}' | interp )* '"'
interp       ::= '{' expr '='? conversion? format-spec? '}'
conversion   ::= '!' ( 'r' | 's' | 'a' )       # r = debug, s = display, a = ascii
format-spec  ::= ':' fmt-char*                  # opaque here; the Format protocol reads it
fmt-char     ::= < any character except '{', '}', or a line break >
fstr-char    ::= < any code point except '"', '\', '{', '}', or a line break >
print        ::= 'print' expr

# ==================================================================================
# 6. Control flow & Pattern matching
# ==================================================================================
# 'for' is a statement (run for effect); 'match' is an expression, and 'if' is BOTH — a
# statement, and an expression when it has an 'else' (if-expr, in 'primary', group 4).
# 'if' takes a bool — no truthiness — or a
# binding head 'if x := expr' that runs the block only when expr is present. 'for' is
# the one loop: infinite, WHILE a condition holds, or over an Iterable ('for mut x'
# binds in place). 'break' / 'continue' act on the nearest 'for'; 'break if c' /
# 'continue if c' are sugar. The iterate form is taken when 'mut' or an 'identifier in'
# follows 'for'; a bare 'for expr' with no 'in' is the while condition. Since 'in' is also
# the membership operator (group 4), a while-loop on membership is parenthesized —
# 'for (v in r) { … }' — so a head-leading 'identifier in' always reads as iterate.

if-stmt      ::= 'if' if-head block ( 'else' 'if' if-head block )* ( 'else' block )?
# An 'if' with a MANDATORY trailing 'else' is also an EXPRESSION (if-expr, in primary,
# group 4): it yields the taken branch's block value, and every branch must yield the
# same type. As a statement (if-stmt) the 'else' is optional and no value is produced;
# at statement position the statement form wins.
if-expr      ::= 'if' if-head block ( 'else' 'if' if-head block )* 'else' block
if-head      ::= expr | identifier ':=' expr           # binding form runs on 'present'
for-stmt     ::= 'for' block                            # infinite (leave via break/return)
               | 'for' 'mut'? identifier 'in' expr block   # iterate an Iterable
               | 'for' expr block                          # while — repeat while expr (a bool) holds
break        ::= 'break' ( 'if' expr )?
continue     ::= 'continue' ( 'if' expr )?

# A bare block just opens a nested scope — its bindings and scope-owned values are
# freed at its '}'. 'with' is sugar over it: it binds a scoped resource for the block
# and guarantees the resource's teardown runs on EVERY exit (normal, return, abort).
# The value implements the built-in 'Scoped' spec (its one method is the teardown); a
# Ref[T]'s drop already satisfies it. So `with acquire() as y { body }` desugars to
# `{ y := acquire(); defer <y's Scoped teardown>; body }`; 'as y' is optional when the
# resource is used only for its scope (e.g. a held lock).
with-stmt    ::= 'with' expr ( 'as' identifier )? block

# 'match' tries a value against arms in order and yields the first fit; every arm
# yields the same type. A trailing '_' covers the rest. An arm may carry a GUARD — an
# 'if expr' after the pattern — that must also hold; the guard sees the pattern's
# bindings and, on 'A | B if c', covers the whole or-pattern. A guarded arm does NOT
# count toward exhaustiveness (the compiler can't prove the guard holds), so a case
# still needs an unguarded arm (or '_') to be covered.
#
# A RANGE ARM ('200..300 ->', '400..=499 ->', '500.. ->') is match-only SUGAR for a guard
# — '_ if _ in <range>' — so it matches by CONTAINMENT (the '..' operators, group 4), not
# 'equal', and inherits a guard's exhaustiveness (it does not count as covering). It does
# NOT bind; to use the value, write the explicit form 'x if x in <range>'. Bounds are
# compile-time constants; a range arm is recognized by its '..'.
match-expr   ::= 'match' expr '{' match-arm+ '}'
match-arm    ::= ( pattern | range-arm ) ( 'if' expr )? '->' expr   # optional guard
range-arm    ::= range-bound ( '..' range-bound? | '..=' range-bound )
range-bound  ::= '-'? literal | identifier          # a compile-time constant bound

# A pattern destructures by copy.
# A bare name in pattern position is a 'variant-pat' when it RESOLVES to a known type or
# enum variant in scope, and a 'binding-pat' (a fresh name) otherwise — the same name
# resolution the postfix grammar uses for index-vs-type-arguments (group 4). Names are
# case-free, so this is decided by resolution, not by capitalization.
pattern      ::= sub-pattern ( '|' sub-pattern )*       # or-pattern binds same names
# A struct pattern must list EVERY field, or end with '..' to ignore the rest ('Div{q, r}'
# lists all; 'Div{q, ..}' ignores the rest; 'Div{..}' matches any). A list pattern may
# hold at most ONE '..' — '..name' binds the skipped run as a list, a bare '..' drops it.
# (struct '..' only ignores; it never binds.) 'pattern' position '..' is REST, distinct
# from the value-level range '..' (group 4).
#
# 'pattern-core as name' also binds the WHOLE matched value to 'name' while the core keeps
# destructuring — 'Move{x, y} as m', 'Some(inner as v)'. 'as' reads the same as in
# 'with'/'import': '<thing> as <name>'. On an or-pattern it binds the nearest alternative
# ('A | B as m' is 'A | (B as m)'); bind both sides with 'A as m | B as m'.
sub-pattern  ::= pattern-core ( 'as' identifier )?
pattern-core ::= variant-pat | struct-pat | tuple-pat | list-pat | literal-pat | binding-pat | '_'
variant-pat  ::= type-name ( '(' pattern-list ')' )?    # Left(v), Some, nested Left(Some(v))
struct-pat   ::= type-name '{' struct-fields? '}'       # Div{q, r}; Div{q, ..}; Div{..}
struct-fields ::= field-pat ( ',' field-pat )* ( ',' '..' )? | '..'
tuple-pat    ::= '(' pattern-list ')'                   # (a, b) — also a bind-target
list-pat     ::= '[' ( list-pat-elem ( ',' list-pat-elem )* )? ']'   # [a, b], [h, ..t], []
list-pat-elem ::= pattern | '..' identifier?            # '..name' binds the rest as a list
literal-pat  ::= '-'? literal                           # matched by equal; '-' only on a number
binding-pat  ::= identifier                             # binds anything, by copy
field-pat    ::= identifier ( ':' pattern )?            # shorthand, or field: pattern
pattern-list ::= pattern ( ',' pattern )*

# ==================================================================================
# 7. Types & Declarations
# ==================================================================================
# The type expressions referenced since group 5, and the declarations that introduce
# types and behavior: 'struct' (product), 'enum' (sum), 'type X = Y' (strong typedef),
# 'spec' (behavioral interface), and 'impl'. Structural derivation is a decorator on
# the type, '#[derive(...)]' (below). A method takes no explicit receiver — 'this' is
# implicit inside a method (see param, group 5); the self type is 'This'.

# --- type expressions ---
# A type-name is just an identifier, so the set of built-in NUMERIC types — 'int',
# 'uint', 'float', and any fixed-width members ('i32', 'u8', 'f64', …) — is a STDLIB
# decision, not surface grammar: adding one adds no production.
type         ::= base-type '?'?                        # a trailing '?' makes it optional
base-type    ::= type-name type-args? ( '.' identifier )*   # int, list[int]; 'I.Item' PROJECTS an
               | tuple-type | array-type | chan-type | fn-type   # associated type — chainable: 'I.Item.Sub'
type-args    ::= '[' type ( ',' type )* ']'
tuple-type   ::= '(' type ( ',' type )+ ')'            # 2+ elements
array-type   ::= '[' type ';' expr ']'                 # [T; N] — the ';' array position
chan-type    ::= 'chan' '[' type ']'           # bidirectional
               | '<-' 'chan' '[' type ']'       # receive-only (a receiver), Go-style
               | 'chan' '[' type ']' '<-'       # send-only (a sender), Go-style

# --- declarations (compound: struct/enum/spec/impl; simple: type) ---
struct-decl  ::= 'pub'? 'struct' identifier generics? '{' field-list? '}'
enum-decl    ::= 'pub'? 'enum' identifier generics? '{' variant-list? '}'
type-decl    ::= 'pub'? 'type' identifier generics? '=' type      # strong typedef
spec-decl    ::= 'pub'? 'spec' identifier generics? ( ':' bound )? '{' spec-member* '}'
impl-decl    ::= 'impl' generics? type-name type-args? 'for' type '{' impl-item* '}'  # a spec impl
             | 'impl' generics? type '{' impl-item* '}'                              # inherent
             # 'impl[T] Spec for list[T]' — the impl's type params sit after 'impl', so a
             # T introduced here is usable in the target. The 'for'-less form adds
             # INHERENT methods to a type (no spec): 'impl User { … }' — a named
             # constructor 'User.from_json(…)' (an associated fn, no 'this') or a private
             # method 'u.recompute()' (uses 'this'). Every method and associated fn on a
             # type shares one namespace, spec or inherent alike; a duplicate is an error.
impl-item    ::= fn-decl | assoc-bind        # a method / associated fn, or an assoc-type binding
assoc-bind   ::= 'type' identifier '=' type  # 'type Item = int' — satisfies a spec's assoc type

# CONSTRUCTION. A type name is ALSO its constructor: 'struct'/'enum' put the name into
# the value namespace as a callable, so 'User(id: 1, name: "x")' builds a struct (its
# fields become parameters in declaration order — positional then named, group 5) and
# 'Circle(3.0)' builds an enum variant. The name is SHARED with functions: a type and a
# function cannot share a name (a duplicate is an error — Zerg has no overloading).
#
# FIELD VISIBILITY & DEFAULTS. A field is 'pub' for external INSTANCE ACCESS (read/write
# of 'u.field'); a non-pub field is module-private, and MUST carry a default. A field's
# default lets its constructor argument be omitted. There are NO zero values: a non-
# optional field with no default is REQUIRED at construction; the ONE implicit default is
# 'nil' for a 'T?' field (its natural absent state). The field-wise 'T(...)' constructor
# is public and PRIMITIVE (not overridable); a custom constructor is a named associated
# function (an inherent 'impl', group 5) that builds via 'T(...)'. The '#[sealed]'
# decorator demotes 'T(...)' to module-private, so external code must use a public custom
# constructor — the module still builds with 'T(...)' internally.

# Decorators are a fixed, compiler-owned set of directives (users cannot define new
# ones — Zerg has no macros). A decorator prefix leads any declaration (the
# 'decorator*' on 'decorated-decl', group 1) and binds to it. 'derive' asks the
# compiler to generate the canonical impls of the named blessed specs by reading the
# type's structure; other decorators (layout, logging, FFI, …) slot in here later.
decorator    ::= '#[' deco-item ( ',' deco-item )* ']'
deco-item    ::= identifier ( '(' deco-arg ( ',' deco-arg )* ')' )?
deco-arg     ::= type-name | literal          # derive(Encode, Decode), align(16), …

# A generic parameter may carry a spec BOUND — 'T: Ord' means every method a body calls
# on a T comes from the named specs (a '+' conjunction: 'T: Ord + Hash' grants both).
# The same 'bound' is a spec's SUPER-SPEC — 'spec Ord: Eq' makes an 'impl Ord' also
# require 'impl Eq', and lets Ord's body use Eq's methods on This. There is NO disjunction
# bound ('T: A | B'): a body could not know which methods T has, so it cannot monomorphize.
# To accept SEVERAL argument types, parameterize a spec and write one impl per type — e.g.
# 'spec Indexable[K] { type Output; fn index(k: K) -> Output }' with 'impl Indexable[int]'
# (element) and 'impl Indexable[Range]' (slice) for a list, so 'xs[k]' resolves statically
# on k's type and each impl keeps its own 'Output' — or use an 'enum' for a runtime choice.
#
# Generics MONOMORPHIZE: each distinct type argument compiles to its own specialized C
# function. So a bound is load-bearing (it names the impl to specialize against, and in
# generic code 'a < b' is valid only if T's bounds provide the operator's spec), and a
# generic function is NOT a first-class value until instantiated. Soundness needs
# COHERENCE (at most one 'impl Spec for Type' program-wide) and an ORPHAN rule (the impl
# must own the spec or the type). Generics are invariant — Zerg has no subtyping. The
# '#[dyn]' decorator instead compiles a generic to one shared witness-table body (a
# size-for-speed trade; see docs). Explicit call-site type arguments are written 'f[T]'
# — disambiguated from an index by name resolution (see postfix, group 4). Const
# generics are deferred.
generics     ::= '[' type-param ( ',' type-param )* ']'
type-param   ::= identifier ( ':' bound )?             # an optional spec bound
bound        ::= type-name ( '+' type-name )*          # a conjunction of specs
# Fields and variants are separated exactly like statements — by stmt-sep (a line
# break, realized as ';' by ASI). There is NO ',' between them; the ',' inside a
# variant payload '(A, B)' is an ordinary argument list.
field-list   ::= stmt-sep* ( field ( stmt-sep+ field )* stmt-sep* )?
field        ::= 'pub'? identifier ':' type ( '=' expr )?   # 'pub' = external access; default feeds ctor
variant-list ::= stmt-sep* ( variant ( stmt-sep+ variant )* stmt-sep* )?
variant      ::= identifier ( '(' type ( ',' type )* ')' )? ( '=' expr )?   # payload or discriminant
# A '= expr' gives a variant an explicit DISCRIMINANT — only when EVERY variant is
# fieldless (a C-style integer enum; a payload enum keeps its tag opaque and match-only).
# The value is a compile-time-constant integer, distinct across variants; an unspecified
# one is the previous + 1 (from 0). Such an enum has a native, C-compatible integer repr
# (backing 'int' by ONE default rule — no annotation needed; 'int(v)' reads the value,
# 'E.from(n) -> E?' reverses). A specific width is an opt-in layout decorator ('#[repr]',
# reserved); the serialized/wire form is the 'Encode'/'Decode' impl, never a decorator.
# A spec member is a required method (signature), a provided method (with a body), or an
# ASSOCIATED TYPE — a type the impl fills in ('type Item'), functionally determined by
# the implementer (one per impl). It makes a single-output protocol like iteration well
# defined: 'for x in it' has ONE element type because 'Iterator.Item' is fixed per impl,
# not chosen per use as a generic 'Iterable[T]' would allow. Reference it by projection
# in type position — 'I.Item' (group 4's base-type). (An associated CONST would need a
# 'const', deferred; associated types are types only for now.)
spec-member  ::= fn-sig | fn-decl | assoc-type         # required / provided method, or assoc type
assoc-type   ::= 'type' identifier ( ':' bound )?      # 'type Item' or bounded 'type Item: Ord'
fn-sig       ::= 'mut'? 'fn' identifier generics? '(' param-list? ')' ret-type?

# ==================================================================================
# 8. Null-safety & Errors
# ==================================================================================
# Failure comes in two tiers. RECOVERABLE failure is an ordinary value of a sum type
# — 'Either[X, Y]', 'Result[T]' = Either[T, Err], and 'T?' = Either[T, nil] with the
# placeholder 'nil'. A BUG is an ABORT that unwinds the stack (running defers). The
# operators bridge the tiers:
#   x?            propagate — unwrap the Left, else early-return the Right from the fn
#   a ?? b        default   — a's Left if present, else b; right-assoc, short-circuits
#   a?.b          optional chain (T? only) — read .b when present, else nil in place
#   x!            force-unwrap — unwrap the Left, else raise UnwrapError (value → abort)
#   raise e       abort with an Err (value → abort); 'raise e from c' records c as cause
#   guard { … }   demote an abort in the block back to a value → Result[T] (abort → value)
# The '??' right side may DIVERGE instead of giving a value — 'x ?? break', 'v ?? return
# nil', 'p ?? raise Err' — because break/continue/return/raise never produce a value.

coalesce-expr ::= or-expr ( '??' coalesce-rhs )?    # '??': loosest binary, right-assoc
coalesce-rhs  ::= coalesce-expr | diverge
diverge       ::= 'break' | 'continue' | return | raise
raise         ::= 'raise' expr ( 'from' expr )?     # 'from c' records c as e's cause
guard-expr    ::= 'guard' block                     # yields Result[T]; T = the block's value

# ==================================================================================
# 9. Concurrency — coroutines & channels
# ==================================================================================
# Concurrency is coroutines + channels only (CSP). 'spawn f(args)' starts a
# fire-and-forget coroutine (no handle, no join). A channel is built with
# 'chan[T](cap?)' (capacity 0 = unbuffered rendezvous); 'ch <- v' sends, '<-ch'
# receives yielding Result[T] (Right = closed, carrying StopIteration or a crash
# Err). A bare 'chan[T]' is bidirectional and narrows to '<-chan[T]' / 'chan[T]<-'
# or via the values 'ch.recv' / 'ch.send'. There is NO explicit close (the channel
# auto-closes when its last sender leaves) and NO 'yield'. 'select' is the only
# multi-way wait.

spawn-stmt   ::= 'spawn' expr                  # fire-and-forget; expr is a call
send-stmt    ::= expr '<-' expr                # 'ch <- v'; send yields no value
chan-new     ::= 'chan' '[' type ']' '(' expr? ')'   # constructor; optional capacity

# 'select' watches several send/receive arms, runs the first ready one (fair ties).
# 'done' fires once when every watched receive channel has closed; '_' fires when
# nothing is ready (non-blocking). Both 'done' and '_' are contextual — special only
# as a select-arm head.
select-stmt  ::= 'select' '{' select-arm+ '}'
select-arm   ::= recv-arm | send-arm | 'done' '->' expr | '_' '->' expr
recv-arm     ::= ( ( identifier | '_' ) ':=' )? '<-' expr '->' expr
send-arm     ::= expr '<-' expr '->' expr

# ==================================================================================
# 10. Modules & Programs
# ==================================================================================
# Source is organized as program > package > module (a directory) > file. Visibility
# is 'pub' (already a prefix on every declaration): a plain declaration is module-
# private, 'pub' exposes it to the rest of the package, and a package's public API is
# the 'pub' surface of its ROOT module. 'import' names another module/package; a
# 'pub import' RE-EXPORTS it onto this module's surface (how a root builds its API).
# 'init()' is lazy module setup, run on first use. A module may declare MULTIPLE 'init()'
# blocks; they run in declaration (FIFO) order, and each runs EXACTLY ONCE. There are NO
# mutable globals: a module-level (top-level) binding may not be 'mut' — a top-level ':='
# is an immutable module constant, evaluated at init. A program is a build rooted at an
# entry file that defines a top-level 'fn main(...) -> Result[nil]' — 'main' is an
# ordinary function, not reserved. 'package' is the distribution / versioning unit: a
# directory tree selected by the build tool, with NO in-source 'package' declaration.
#
# An 'import' binds a NAMESPACE: the path's LAST segment ('import util/text' binds
# 'text'), reached with '.' — 'text.split(…)'. 'as' renames it ('import a/text as at'),
# which is how two imports that share a last segment coexist. The bound name lives in
# the one value namespace (group 7), so colliding with a local name is an error — rename
# with 'as'. There is NO selective ('from … import …') or glob import: to use a member
# unqualified, bind it locally — 'split := text.split' — since a function is a value.
import-stmt  ::= 'pub'? 'import' import-path ( 'as' identifier )?
import-path  ::= identifier ( '/' identifier )*      # a module (directory) path
init-decl    ::= 'init' '(' ')' block                # lazy setup; many allowed, FIFO, each once

# ==================================================================================
# 11. Resource cleanup — defer & del
# ==================================================================================
# Three constructs share one axis — WHEN cleanup fires. 'defer expr' runs expr at the
# enclosing block's exit, on EVERY path out (normal, return, or an abort unwind), in
# last-scheduled-first order. 'del name' revokes that name's access to its storage
# NOW; the storage is freed only if the revoked access was the owning one and no other
# holder remains (a Ref[T]/chan drops a refcount instead — 'del ch' closes a channel
# if you were its last sender). ('Ref[T]' drop, the third point on the axis, fires at
# the last holder's scope exit — not a statement.)
defer-stmt   ::= 'defer' expr            # run at block exit, every path incl. abort
del-stmt     ::= 'del' identifier        # revoke a name's access now

